  insert into public.course_items (course_id, kind, position, title, content) select cid, 'topic', 1, $j$1.1 Welcome Back: Present Tense and Question Review$j$, $j${"readings": [{"title": "Proverbs 1 (King James Version)", "url": "/portal/bible/Proverbs/1"}], "body": "Proverbs 1:5 says, A wise man will hear, and will increase learning. Spanish 2 builds on what you already know, so we begin by reviewing the present tense. Regular verbs follow patterns. For ar verbs the endings are o, as, a, amos and an, so hablar becomes hablo, hablas, habla, hablamos, hablan. For er verbs the endings are o, es, e, emos and en, so comer becomes como, comes, come, comemos, comen. For ir verbs the endings are o, es, e, imos and en, so vivir becomes vivo, vives, vive, vivimos, viven.\n\nThree verbs you must know by heart are ser, estar and tener. Ser is soy, eres, es, somos, son and tells who or what someone is. Estar is estoy, estás, está, estamos, están and tells where someone is and how they feel. Tener is tengo, tienes, tiene, tenemos, tienen and also tells age, as in Tengo dieciséis años.\n\nQuestion words are qué, quién, dónde, cuándo, por qué, cómo, cuánto and cuál. Each has an accent mark, and each question begins with ¿ and ends with ?. Worked example: ¿Dónde vives? Vivo en Pompano Beach. ¿Cuándo estudias? Estudio por la tarde. Common mistake: leaving out the accent on question words, which changes the meaning of por qué (why) and porque (because).\n\nYour task: (1) Conjugate trabajar, aprender and escribir. (2) Write six questions with six different question words and answer each in Spanish.\n\nFlorida standards: Florida World Languages standards, Level 2 (WL.K12.NH, WL.K12.IL).", "video": "", "presenter": "Dr. Alex L. Jenkins Sr.", "standards": "Florida World Languages standards, Level 2 (WL.K12.NH, WL.K12.IL)", "script": "Welcome back to Spanish 2. Listen, Proverbs 1:5 says a wise man will hear, and will increase learning. We begin with a review.\n\nNow watch this. Ar verbs take o, as, a, amos, an. Hablo, hablas, habla, hablamos, hablan. Er verbs take o, es, e, emos, en. Ir verbs take o, es, e, imos, en.\n\nRemember ser, estar and tener. Soy, estoy, tengo. Ser tells who you are, estar tells where you are and how you feel, and tener tells your age.\n\nQuestion words carry accents: qué, quién, dónde, cuándo, por qué, cómo, cuánto, cuál. Do not confuse por qué with porque.\n\nYour task is to conjugate three verbs and write six questions with answers. Take the four question check. Excellence, Faith, Purpose. I will see you in the next lesson."}$j$::jsonb where not exists (select 1 from public.course_items where course_id = cid and kind = 'topic' and title = $j$1.1 Welcome Back: Present Tense and Question Review$j$);
  insert into public.course_items (course_id, kind, position, title, content) select cid, 'topic', 2, $j$1.2 Reflexive Verbs and the Daily Routine$j$, $j${"readings": [{"title": "Psalm 5 (King James Version)", "url": "/portal/bible/Psalms/5"}], "body": "Psalm 5:3 says, My voice shalt thou hear in the morning, O LORD; in the morning will I direct my prayer unto thee, and will look up. A morning routine is a good place to practice reflexive verbs. A reflexive verb describes an action that a person does to himself. The infinitive ends in se, as in levantarse, to get up.\n\nUse a reflexive pronoun before the conjugated verb: me, te, se, nos, se. Levantarse becomes me levanto, te levantas, se levanta, nos levantamos, se levantan. Other useful verbs are despertarse (to wake up), ducharse (to shower), lavarse (to wash oneself), cepillarse los dientes (to brush one's teeth), peinarse (to comb one's hair), vestirse (to get dressed) and acostarse (to go to bed).\n\nSome are stem changing. Despertarse changes e to ie: me despierto. Vestirse changes e to i: me visto. Acostarse changes o to ue: me acuesto.\n\nWorked example: Me despierto a las seis, me ducho, me visto y oro antes de desayunar. Con un infinitivo, the pronoun may go before the verb or attach to the infinitive: Voy a acostarme or Me voy a acostar. Common mistake: using the English pattern, I wash my hands, with mi. In Spanish say Me lavo las manos, using the article las.\n\nYour task: (1) Write the full present tense of lavarse. (2) Describe your morning in six Spanish sentences.\n\nFlorida standards: Florida World Languages standards, Level 2 (WL.K12.NH, WL.K12.IL).", "video": "", "presenter": "Dr. Alex L. Jenkins Sr.", "standards": "Florida World Languages standards, Level 2 (WL.K12.NH, WL.K12.IL)", "script": "Welcome back. Listen, Psalm 5:3 says, My voice shalt thou hear in the morning, O LORD. Let us talk about the morning routine.\n\nNow watch this. A reflexive verb ends in se. The pronouns are me, te, se, nos, se. Me levanto, te levantas, se levanta, nos levantamos, se levantan.\n\nUseful verbs are despertarse, ducharse, lavarse, vestirse and acostarse. Some change their stems. Me despierto, me visto, me acuesto.\n\nSay it this way: Me lavo las manos. Do not say mi manos. Spanish uses the article with body parts and clothing.\n\nWith an infinitive you may say Voy a acostarme or Me voy a acostar.\n\nYour task is to conjugate lavarse and describe your morning in six sentences. Take the four question check. Excellence, Faith, Purpose. I will see you in the next lesson."}$j$::jsonb where not exists (select 1 from public.course_items where course_id = cid and kind = 'topic' and title = $j$1.2 Reflexive Verbs and the Daily Routine$j$);
end
$seed$;
